---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import StatusSpec from '../components/StatusSpec.astro';
import LearnLoop from '../components/LearnLoop.astro';
import Leleu from '../components/Leleu.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config.mjs';

const ladder = `Platform Engineering
        ↓
      MLOps
        ↓
       ML
        ↓
       AI
        ↓
       WTF`;
---

<Base
  title="About the Engineer"
  description="Why a Platform Engineer decided to learn MLOps and AI in public, and what this journal is for."
>
  <div class="page">
    <header class="article-head">
      <h1 class="article-head__title">About the Engineer</h1>
      <p class="article-head__stamp">Why I’m doing this</p>
    </header>

    <div class="article-layout">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          I’m {SITE.author}, a Platform Engineer / SRE. I’ve spent my career on the boring, load-bearing
          side of software: distributed systems, cloud, Kubernetes, CI/CD, observability, incident
          response, and the long tail of infrastructure that only becomes visible when it fails.
        </p>

        <p>
          I’m good at making systems <em>run</em>. I know how to make something reliable, observable
          and recoverable at 3am. What I don’t know (not really, not beyond the vocabulary) is how
          to make a system <em>think</em>.
        </p>

        <blockquote>
          <p>I know infrastructure. I don’t know ML. Let’s see what happens.</p>
        </blockquote>

        <h2>What I’m moving into</h2>

        <p>Deliberately, and in public:</p>

        <ul>
          <li>Machine learning fundamentals: enough to stop nodding politely</li>
          <li>MLOps: pipelines, registries, reproducibility, drift</li>
          <li>LLMs, retrieval, evaluation</li>
          <li>Agents, and where they actually earn their complexity</li>
          <li>Model serving and inference infrastructure</li>
          <li>AI infrastructure: GPUs, schedulers, cost, latency</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          Some of that is closer to my current job than it looks. A model registry is an artifact
          repository with opinions. A feature store is a cache with a consistency problem. A training
          run is a batch job that lies about its resource usage.
        </p>

        <p>
          And some of it is genuinely new to me, which is the whole point of writing it down.
        </p>

        <h2>What this site is</h2>

        <p>
          An engineering journal. Not a tutorial series, not a course, not advice. Each entry is a
          log of something I tried, what I expected, what actually happened, and what I understood
          afterwards, including the entries where the answer is “I still don’t know”.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you read it in order, you should be able to watch me get less wrong over time. That’s
          the only metric this site has.
        </p>

        <h2>What this site is not</h2>

        <p>
          I’m not an AI expert. I’m not going to tell you which framework to adopt, predict what
          happens to your job, or explain transformers with an analogy involving a restaurant. There
          are enough people doing that.
        </p>

        <h2>The route</h2>

        <pre>{ladder}</pre>

        <p>
          The last step isn’t a joke about giving up. It’s the honest label for the part of the map
          I haven’t drawn yet, and, so far, the part where most of the learning happens.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="toc" aria-label="Status">
        <p class="toc__title">Status</p>
        <StatusSpec compact />
        <p class="toc__title" style="margin-top:2.5rem">How I learn</p>
        <LearnLoop />
        <Leleu width="11rem" class="about__leleu" />
        <p class="mono-label" style="margin-top:0.75rem">
          Leleu: permanently mid-metamorphosis, regrows what he breaks. The mascot picked itself.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
